<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mark Antony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/83-bce">83</a>–<a href="/years/30-bce">30 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mark Antony was a Roman general and politician, an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and later a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a>. His alliance and relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a> bound him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>. Their defeat by Octavian at Actium in 31 BCE led to the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Octavian's establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mark-antony.jpg" alt="Mark Antony" width="320" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Byzantium565 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octavian_and_Antony_denarius_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 36 BCE Publius Canidius Crassus, a general of <b>Mark Antony</b>, campaigned against the Iberians and Albanians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mark Antony</b> invaded and defeated the kingdom in 34 BCE, but Rome lost hegemony in the Final War of the Republic, 32–30 BCE, and in 20 BCE Augustus negotiated a truce with the Parthians that left Armenia a buffer zone between the two powers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…effect at Carrhae in 53 BCE, where a Parthian army under the general Surena, supplied with arrows by a camel train, destroyed a Roman army led by Marcus Licinius Crassus, who was killed in the aftermath; <b>Mark Antony</b>’s invasion of 36 BCE also ended in a costly retreat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>She sought to preserve Egyptian independence through alliances with Julius Caesar and <b>Mark Antony</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Egypt came under Roman rule at the end of the civil war between Octavian and <b>Mark Antony</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Julius Caesar's crossing of the Rubicon in 49 BCE, his assassination in 44 BCE and the wars of the Second Triumvirate ended in Octavian's decisive victory over <b>Mark Antony</b> and Cleopatra VII at the Battle of Actium in 31 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-49" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mark Antony"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-49">What was here in 49 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for I shall soon be with you; but it distresses me that so great a general should be found of a tardier courage than a woman.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Antony &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; believing Cleopatra already dead, before he took his own life; Plutarch's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
